                    <span>C</span><span>o</span><span>n</span><span>f</span><span>e</span><span>r</span><span>e</span><span>n</span><span>c</span><span>e</span
                    ><span>s</span>
                </span>
            </h2>
            <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8">
                <!-- Microsoft Build -->
                <div class="project-card">
                    <div class="mb-4">
                        <div class="w-full h-48 bg-gradient-to-br from-primary/30 to-secondary/30 rounded-lg flex items-center justify-center">
                            <span class="text-4xl">🏢</span>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <h3 class="mb-4 gradient-text">Microsoft Build</h3>
                    <p class="mb-4 text-text-muted">AI & Cloud • Developer Tools • Azure Integration</p>
                    <p class="mb-6">
                        "AI-Powered Observability: The Future of Application Monitoring" - Exploring how AI transforms monitoring and troubleshooting.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <!-- SCaLE 22x -->
                <div class="project-card">
                    <div class="mb-4">
                        <div class="w-full h-48 bg-gradient-to-br from-secondary/30 to-accent/30 rounded-lg flex items-center justify-center">
                            <span class="text-4xl">🐧</span>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <h3 class="mb-4 gradient-text-secondary">SCaLE 22x</h3>
                    <p class="mb-4 text-text-muted">Open Source • Linux • Community</p>
                    <p class="mb-6">
                        "Open Source Observability: Building Monitoring That Scales" - Best practices for implementing observability in open source
                        environments.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <!-- Google Next -->
                <div class="project-card">
                    <div class="mb-4">
                        <div class="w-full h-48 bg-gradient-to-br from-accent/30 to-primary/30 rounded-lg flex items-center justify-center">
                            <span class="text-4xl">☁️</span>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <h3 class="mb-4 gradient-text">Google Next</h3>
                    <p class="mb-4 text-text-muted">Cloud Native • GCP • Kubernetes</p>
                    <p class="mb-6">
                        "Observability in the Cloud-Native Era" - Strategies for monitoring microservices and containerized applications at scale.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <!-- AWS re:Invent -->
                <div class="project-card">
                    <div class="mb-4">
                        <div class="w-full aspect-video bg-gradient-to-br from-primary/20 to-accent/20 rounded-lg overflow-hidden shadow-xl">
                            <iframe
                                class="w-full h-full"
                                src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/6kK27w0xPMQ"
                                title="AWS re:Invent - Platform Engineering: Building a Platform Developers Love"
                                style="border: none;"
                                allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share; fullscreen"
                            ></iframe>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <h3 class="mb-4 gradient-text-secondary">AWS re:Invent</h3>
                    <p class="mb-4 text-text-muted">Platform Engineering • Developer Experience • Infrastructure</p>
                    <p class="mb-6">
                        Platform engineering isn't just rebranded CI/CD. Successful platforms provide more than tools; they foster developer autonomy. In this session, hear a lead platform engineer from Starbucks and an advocacy manager from Datadog share advice for building a platform that developers love. Learn key features required to accelerate production deployments, improve reliability, and control costs so developer teams can have true service ownership. This presentation is brought to you by Datadog, an AWS Partner.
                    </p>
                    <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4">
                        <a 
                            href="https://youtu.be/6kK27w0xPMQ?si=z1T84MDhtGS1JhuX" 
                            target="_blank" 
                            rel="noopener noreferrer" 
                            class="btn btn-sm"
                        >
                            Watch on YouTube
                        </a>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- DevOpsDays Denver -->
                <div class="project-card">
                    <div class="mb-4">
                        <div class="w-full aspect-video bg-gradient-to-br from-secondary/20 to-primary/20 rounded-lg overflow-hidden shadow-xl">
                            <iframe
                                class="w-full h-full"
                                src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/r4ePTRIsFoA"
                                title="DevOpsDays Denver - Building a Culture of Observability"
                                style="border: none;"
                                allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share; fullscreen"
                            ></iframe>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <h3 class="mb-4 gradient-text">DevOpsDays Denver</h3>
                    <p class="mb-4 text-text-muted">DevOps Culture • Automation • Collaboration</p>
                    <p class="mb-6">"Building a Culture of Observability" - How to foster observability practices across development and operations teams.</p>
                    <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4">
                        <a 
                            href="https://youtu.be/r4ePTRIsFoA?si=T-6tQGhJegWTxS_o" 
                            target="_blank" 
                            rel="noopener noreferrer" 
                            class="btn btn-sm"
                        >
                            Watch on YouTube
                        </a>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- .NET Conf -->
                <div class="project-card">
                    <div class="mb-4">
                        <div class="w-full aspect-video bg-gradient-to-br from-accent/20 to-secondary/20 rounded-lg overflow-hidden shadow-xl">
                            <iframe
                                class="w-full h-full"
                                src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/4xMIROBECRw"
                                title=".NET Conf - Monitoring .NET Applications: From Development to Production"
                                style="border: none;"
                                allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share; fullscreen"
                            ></iframe>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <h3 class="mb-4 gradient-text-secondary">.NET Conf</h3>
                    <p class="mb-4 text-text-muted">.NET • Application Performance • Monitoring</p>
                    <p class="mb-6">In this session, we will show you how to instrument a .NET Blazor application to use OpenTelemetry and .NET Aspire to help us visualize what is really happening in our application. We will than see how we can add OpenTelemetry exporters and use different solution: Azure Application Insight or Datadog APM to collect those OpenTelemetry metrics and traces and how it could help us.</p>
                    <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4">
                        <a 
                            href="https://youtu.be/4xMIROBECRw?si=kBXP0VjayHB6jrQq" 
                            target="_blank" 
                            rel="noopener noreferrer" 
                            class="btn btn-sm btn-secondary"
                        >
                            Watch on YouTube
                        </a>
                    </div>
